I grew up in Suyu-ri.

245
00:08:40,390 --> 00:08:41,959
- That's close from here.
- Right. It's nearby.

246
00:08:41,960 --> 00:08:44,299
Hagye-dong, Sanggye-dong, and Junggye-dong.

247
00:08:44,300 --> 00:08:45,359
- That's our turf.
- And...

248
00:08:45,360 --> 00:08:48,199
there was Jung Sang No Dance School in front of Nowon Station.

249
00:08:48,200 --> 00:08:50,740
When I was in elementary school, I went to the dance school...

250
00:08:50,870 --> 00:08:52,870
and trained to get into an arts middle school.

251
00:08:53,010 --> 00:08:54,210
That's how...

252
00:08:54,410 --> 00:08:55,709
- You went to an arts middle school?
- Yes.

253
00:08:55,710 --> 00:08:57,179
- As a dance major?
- Yes.

254
00:08:57,180 --> 00:08:58,439
- We moved to a different area.
- That's how you debuted...

255
00:08:58,440 --> 00:08:59,880
- as an idol star.
- She debuted as an idol star.

256
00:09:01,210 --> 00:09:02,280
I shouldn't have brought that up.

257
00:09:02,650 --> 00:09:04,519
- It was M.I.L.K.
- Yes.

258
00:09:04,520 --> 00:09:05,819
- So you grew up around here.
- Yes.

259
00:09:05,820 --> 00:09:07,220
- Right.
- I see.

260
00:09:07,520 --> 00:09:09,419
Anyway, let's go...

261
00:09:09,420 --> 00:09:11,290
- Let's do that. Yes.
- and meet our Whenever Guests.

262
00:09:11,960 --> 00:09:13,229
- What's that?
- My gosh.

263
00:09:13,230 --> 00:09:14,590
- It's pretty long.
- We have a lot of cards now.

264
00:09:15,630 --> 00:09:17,330
Our first Whenever Guest is Kim Myung Seon.

265
00:09:17,800 --> 00:09:20,229
"Our company offers great benefits."

266
00:09:20,230 --> 00:09:21,899
- What?
- "And the best benefit is..."

267
00:09:21,900 --> 00:09:24,070
"that we get to see the owner's face every day."

268
00:09:24,570 --> 00:09:26,539
- "I bring a boxed meal..."
- What's this about?

269
00:09:26,540 --> 00:09:28,509
"every day for my crush, the owner."

270
00:09:28,510 --> 00:09:29,610
- What?
- What is this about?

271
00:09:30,210 --> 00:09:32,139
- Her crush?
- "Sometimes,"

272
00:09:32,140 --> 00:09:34,049
"he doesn't finish his food, which annoys me."

273
00:09:34,050 --> 00:09:37,749
"Is this love or hate? Could you diagnose what I have?"

274
00:09:37,750 --> 00:09:39,989
How can seeing the owner's face be one of the benefits?

275
00:09:39,990 --> 00:09:41,019
- Are they married?
- That's...

276
00:09:41,020 --> 00:09:42,519
- I'm sorry to say this.
- Is that a good thing?

277
00:09:42,520 --> 00:09:44,460
Not seeing the owner sounds more like a benefit.

278
00:09:45,020 --> 00:09:47,559
Is he the owner and her husband?

279
00:09:47,560 --> 00:09:50,799
Even if they are indeed married, this would still be...

280
00:09:50,800 --> 00:09:51,960
a unique relationship.

281
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:53,000
(What's her relationship with the owner?)

282
00:09:53,001 --> 00:09:54,269
- She packs a boxed meal every day.
- This isn't common.

283
00:09:54,270 --> 00:09:55,530
Doing all this...

284
00:09:55,870 --> 00:09:58,270
- Unless they're married...
- Or is it her son?

285
00:09:58,970 --> 00:10:00,169
Or newlyweds.

286
00:10:00,170 --> 00:10:01,769
- It could be her son.
- He could be her son.

287
00:10:01,770 --> 00:10:05,439
She must work for her son's company or something.

288
00:10:05,440 --> 00:10:07,249
- That's it.
- She packs a boxed meal...

289
00:10:07,250 --> 00:10:09,049
- Really?
- in case he gets hungry.

290
00:10:09,050 --> 00:10:10,180
- But he doesn't finish the food.
- Yes.

291
00:10:10,280 --> 00:10:11,379
I see. That must be it.

292
00:10:11,380 --> 00:10:13,490
- Her son is the owner?
- That's possible.

293
00:10:13,820 --> 00:10:16,159
- Gosh, the mother...
- I see.

294
00:10:16,160 --> 00:10:17,459
- Oh, my.
- Is that a benefit?

295
00:10:17,460 --> 00:10:18,560
That would be uncomfortable.

296
00:10:20,430 --> 00:10:23,599
Well, it doesn't matter if it's her son or daughter.

297
00:10:23,600 --> 00:10:25,030
That would make me uncomfortable.

298
00:10:25,630 --> 00:10:27,500
- We have to get on a bus.
- We have to?

299
00:10:27,600 --> 00:10:29,200
- Shall we get going?
- Yes. Let's go.

300
00:10:29,670 --> 00:10:31,869
There should be a town shuttle bus stop right here.

301
00:10:31,870 --> 00:10:33,040
- We're taking the shuttle bus?
- Yes.

302
00:10:33,510 --> 00:10:34,510
(They're all heading to the bus stop.)

303
00:10:34,511 --> 00:10:37,340
Yeon Seok's car is like five steps away from here.

304
00:10:38,240 --> 00:10:39,380
Should we take my car?

305
00:10:39,680 --> 00:10:41,310
His car is right here.

306
00:10:41,450 --> 00:10:42,619
- Gosh.
- We can take my car...

307
00:10:42,620 --> 00:10:43,749
as long as it's okay for our show.

308
00:10:43,750 --> 00:10:44,779
(Yeon Seok drove his car today.)

309
00:10:44,780 --> 00:10:47,090
- It's right there.
- We have to wait for seven minutes.

310
00:10:47,590 --> 00:10:48,590
Seven minutes?

311
00:10:49,360 --> 00:10:50,960
- Your car is right there.
- Let's take...

312
00:10:51,390 --> 00:10:52,390
That's amazing.

313
00:10:52,860 --> 00:10:56,429
I'm going on a school retreat with my students today.

314
00:10:56,430 --> 00:10:58,159
- It's today?
- Students?

315
00:10:58,160 --> 00:10:59,969
- Wait. From the university?
- Yes, from the university.

316
00:10:59,970 --> 00:11:01,199
- From the university?
- Yes.

317
00:11:01,200 --> 00:11:02,399
- I see.
- From your class?

318
00:11:02,400 --> 00:11:03,469
- Yes.
- Your pupils?

319
00:11:03,470 --> 00:11:05,470
- He's a professor.
- They asked me to come.

320
00:11:05,940 --> 00:11:07,140
- That's amazing.
- After this...

321
00:11:07,770 --> 00:11:09,879
Where are your students going for the retreat?

322
00:11:09,880 --> 00:11:11,479
- Chuncheon.
- Gosh.

323
00:11:11,480 --> 00:11:12,779
- Isn't it right here?
- That sounds fun.

324
00:11:12,780 --> 00:11:14,209
- Gosh, Chuncheon?
- There will be traffic later.

325
00:11:14,210 --> 00:11:15,349
- Yes.
- The traffic will be crazy.

326
00:11:15,350 --> 00:11:17,319
- Chuncheon.
- Well,

327
00:11:17,320 --> 00:11:18,519
I bought some presents.

328
00:11:18,520 --> 00:11:19,919
- Really?
- We'll do a random draw.

329
00:11:19,920 --> 00:11:20,949
(Professor Ahn prepared a surprise event.)

330
00:11:20,950 --> 00:11:22,219
- He likes doing that.
- So you can play games.

331
00:11:22,220 --> 00:11:23,619
- Wait. That's...
- My students prepared games.

332
00:11:23,620 --> 00:11:26,490
- He loves playing games.
- He loves playing games.

333
00:11:26,830 --> 00:11:28,289
Whenever we had our get-togethers, he wanted to play...

334
00:11:28,290 --> 00:11:29,759
- rock-paper-scissors.
- He kept suggesting that.

335
00:11:29,760 --> 00:11:31,829
- It was giving gambling vibes.
- Why...

336
00:11:31,830 --> 00:11:33,499
I play way too many games on this show.

337
00:11:33,500 --> 00:11:34,999
(He became a game addict in five seasons.)

338
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:36,699
And when we had get-togethers...

339
00:11:36,700 --> 00:11:38,369
- No, it was when we were filming.
- Yes.

340
00:11:38,370 --> 00:11:40,309
- The games from our show...
- Did you play them?

341
00:11:40,310 --> 00:11:41,310
Yes. The water bottle...

342
00:11:41,311 --> 00:11:43,409
It had a bit of water, and we had to make it stand upright.

343
00:11:43,410 --> 00:11:45,039
- The loser had to buy ice cream.
- Right.

344
00:11:45,040 --> 00:11:46,450
We also played one with the lid.

345
00:11:46,680 --> 00:11:49,119
- It couldn't fall off the table.
- You played that game too?

346
00:11:49,120 --> 00:11:51,219
The loser had to buy ice cream again.

347
00:11:51,220 --> 00:11:53,150
People didn't drink because they had to play games.

348
00:11:53,350 --> 00:11:54,650
They had to be sober for it.

349
00:11:55,720 --> 00:11:57,919
(Thanks to that, they're ready to play games.)

350
00:11:57,920 --> 00:12:00,029
So you're going on a retreat...

351
00:12:00,030 --> 00:12:01,459
- Yes.
- with your students?

352
00:12:01,460 --> 00:12:02,559
- Yes.
- I see.

353
00:12:02,560 --> 00:12:03,929
So they call you Professor there.

354
00:12:03,930 --> 00:12:05,399
But professors don't usually join them.

355
00:12:05,400 --> 00:12:06,829
Isn't that only for the students?

356
00:12:06,830 --> 00:12:09,539
- Professors usually rest...
- I didn't volunteer to go.

357
00:12:09,540 --> 00:12:10,639
- Even if they invited you...
- You're not going...

358
00:12:10,640 --> 00:12:11,739
- when you're not wanted. Right?
- Wait. So...

359
00:12:11,740 --> 00:12:13,639
- Maybe, they were just being nice.
- The students...

360
00:12:13,640 --> 00:12:15,669
- They asked him just to be nice.
- But did you...

361
00:12:15,670 --> 00:12:16,939
- "I'll bring presents then."
- Is that it?

362
00:12:16,940 --> 00:12:17,940
Is that what happened?

363
00:12:18,440 --> 00:12:19,649
- No.
- Hey. You're not lying, right?

364
00:12:19,650 --> 00:12:21,109
No, they asked me to come.

365
00:12:21,110 --> 00:12:22,479
- I mean, sure.
- Hello.

366
00:12:22,480 --> 00:12:23,819
- They can't tell you not to come.
- Listen.

367
00:12:23,820 --> 00:12:25,049
- They're your students.
- I'm not going to stay long.

368
00:12:25,050 --> 00:12:26,889
- Right.
- I'll give them the presents.

369
00:12:26,890 --> 00:12:28,919
- I'll grill the meat.
- Okay.

370
00:12:28,920 --> 00:12:30,090
When will you leave?

371
00:12:30,260 --> 00:12:31,359
Tell us the time.

372
00:12:31,360 --> 00:12:33,790
I'll leave before sundown. I mean, I'll be there after sundown.

373
00:12:34,030 --> 00:12:35,559
- Not until...
- What? Until sunrise?

374
00:12:35,560 --> 00:12:37,429
- Wait. Are you going to stay...
- Until sunrise.

375
00:12:37,430 --> 00:12:38,659
Until 2am or 3am when you're not wanted?

376
00:12:38,660 --> 00:12:40,530
- Can't I leave after dinner?
- I see. After dinner.

377
00:12:40,800 --> 00:12:43,199
(He's just enjoying this.)

378
00:12:43,200 --> 00:12:45,039
- Can't I leave after dinner?
- I see. After dinner.

379
00:12:45,040 --> 00:12:47,510
- Yes. He should eat dinner.
- Yes.

380
00:12:47,610 --> 00:12:49,479
I'm sure they want to spend time by themselves.

381
00:12:49,480 --> 00:12:50,480
(Luckily, the retreat was a big hit.)

382
00:12:50,481 --> 00:12:51,540
We have to get on that.

383
00:12:51,980 --> 00:12:53,810
- That one?
- Bus 11? It's coming.

384
00:12:54,010 --> 00:12:55,609
- Hello.
- Four people, please.

385
00:12:55,610 --> 00:12:57,149
- Hello.
- Hello.

386
00:12:57,150 --> 00:12:58,279
- Four adults, please.
- Hello.

387
00:12:58,280 --> 00:12:59,920
- How many stops do we have to go?
- It's pretty close.

388
00:13:00,420 --> 00:13:02,820
We have to get off at Exit One of Sanggye Station.

389
00:13:03,260 --> 00:13:04,919
- Exit 1 of Sanggye Station.
- That's 6 stops.

390
00:13:04,920 --> 00:13:06,190
- Six stops?
- Yes.

391
00:13:06,330 --> 00:13:07,759
Today is Hangeul Day.

392
00:13:07,760 --> 00:13:09,259
- Today is Hangeul Day.
- Yes.

393
00:13:09,260 --> 00:13:11,900
What do you say? Should we avoid using English words today?

394
00:13:12,470 --> 00:13:13,669
(Suggesting a mini game)

395
00:13:13,670 --> 00:13:15,700
- Let's do that during our meal.
- Seriously.

396
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:18,469
- Seriously. He's all about games.
- He loves playing games.

397
00:13:18,470 --> 00:13:20,670
- It's Hangeul Day.
- We used one right away, "game."

398
00:13:21,870 --> 00:13:23,479
You're right. We already failed.

399
00:13:23,480 --> 00:13:24,810
Yes, you guys already failed.

400
00:13:25,410 --> 00:13:27,679
I'm hesitant to suggest anything right now.

401
00:13:27,680 --> 00:13:28,810
(Hyun Jin and Chun Hee are serious about games.)

402
00:13:28,910 --> 00:13:31,519
Wait. Is there any type of food you prefer for lunch?

403
00:13:31,520 --> 00:13:32,789
I'm good with anything.

404
00:13:32,790 --> 00:13:33,790
What about you?

405
00:13:33,791 --> 00:13:35,089
- Let's eat whatever you want.
- I'm good too.

406
00:13:35,090 --> 00:13:38,590
- I'm bringing this up for a reason.
- What is it?

407
00:13:39,481 --> 00:13:40,481
What about you?

408
00:13:40,482 --> 00:13:41,720
- Let's eat whatever you want.
- I'm good too.

409
00:13:41,721 --> 00:13:45,390
- I'm bringing this up for a reason.
- What is it?

410
00:13:45,391 --> 00:13:46,961
Do you know about the Eight Constitutions?

411
00:13:48,061 --> 00:13:50,225
- Why are you laughing?
- That again.

412
00:13:50,591 --> 00:13:52,730
(What is that?)

413
00:13:52,731 --> 00:13:54,060
- I am...
- The Eight Constitutions...

414
00:13:54,061 --> 00:13:57,130
an avid believer of the Eight Constitutions.

415
00:13:57,131 --> 00:13:59,740
Well, that sounded a bit too strong. It's not blind faith or anything.

416
00:13:59,741 --> 00:14:02,140
- No, that sounds about right.
- That's...

417
00:14:02,141 --> 00:14:03,940
So you want to pick lunch based on the Eight Constitutions?

418
00:14:03,941 --> 00:14:05,670
Hear me out.

419
00:14:05,671 --> 00:14:07,410
- It works pretty well for me.
- Go on.

420
00:14:07,411 --> 00:14:10,380
- She's following that diet.
- I've been following the diet.

421
00:14:10,381 --> 00:14:11,710
Then what do you need to eat?

422
00:14:11,711 --> 00:14:14,121
- What is it?
- I was told to eat temple food.

423
00:14:14,221 --> 00:14:17,720
Rice, seafood, and vegetables. That's it.

424
00:14:17,721 --> 00:14:19,150
- Meat doesn't suit her.
- Without a lot of seasonings.

425
00:14:19,151 --> 00:14:21,190
- No flour. No meat.
- Flour and meat don't suit her.

426
00:14:21,191 --> 00:14:22,360
- I see.
- Yes.

427
00:14:22,361 --> 00:14:24,490
- So if you don't mind, - Yes.

428
00:14:24,491 --> 00:14:26,760
I thought we could get Korean set meals.

429
00:14:26,761 --> 00:14:28,430
- That sounds good.
- Meals with fish.

430
00:14:28,431 --> 00:14:29,770
- Yes. Grilled fish.
- That's good.

431
00:14:29,771 --> 00:14:31,531
I wanted to eat jjajangmyeon, but I'll hold back my craving.

432
00:14:33,477 --> 00:14:36,147
- Today... No.
- We have different constitutions.

433
00:14:36,171 --> 00:14:39,680
Ten years ago, we filmed "Dr. Romantic" together.

434
00:14:39,681 --> 00:14:43,311
I followed her diet then and didn't eat meat for a while.

435
00:14:43,781 --> 00:14:45,810
My mom scolded me a lot.

436
00:14:45,811 --> 00:14:47,880
- Because you didn't eat meat?
- "You brat."

437
00:14:47,881 --> 00:14:50,221
- Because you were picky?
- "I fed you beef-bone soup."

438
00:14:50,551 --> 00:14:52,790
"You enjoyed the soup. What are you talking about?"

439
00:14:52,791 --> 00:14:54,560
My mom did tell me that I used to eat...

440
00:14:54,561 --> 00:14:56,591
pork belly for breakfast when I was in middle school.

441
00:14:57,391 --> 00:15:00,201
Let's try to follow the Eight Constitutions for lunch today.

442
00:15:00,861 --> 00:15:02,600
(Grateful)

443
00:15:02,601 --> 00:15:04,231
We just have to walk a little bit.

444
00:15:04,531 --> 00:15:05,770
Everyone is getting off here.

445
00:15:05,771 --> 00:15:06,901
(Getting off at Sanggye Station)

446
00:15:07,071 --> 00:15:09,411
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

447
00:15:10,441 --> 00:15:11,610
Something smells good.

448
00:15:11,611 --> 00:15:12,781
What is it?

449
00:15:13,341 --> 00:15:14,940
- Oh, my.
- I can smell the fruits.

450
00:15:14,941 --> 00:15:15,980
(A sweet scent of fruit wafts through the street.)

451
00:15:15,981 --> 00:15:17,480
Ten dollars for seven apples!

452
00:15:17,481 --> 00:15:18,710
(Surprised)

453
00:15:18,711 --> 00:15:19,850
(Oh, my!)

454
00:15:19,851 --> 00:15:20,980
- Hello.
- Ten dollars...

455
00:15:20,981 --> 00:15:22,380
- for seven apples. Have a good day.
- Hello.

456
00:15:22,381 --> 00:15:23,791
- He's so young.
- Go straight?

457
00:15:24,221 --> 00:15:26,091
- Ten dollars for seven apples!
- Ten dollars for seven apples!

458
00:15:26,661 --> 00:15:27,661
He has...

459
00:15:28,061 --> 00:15:29,161
a great voice.

460
00:15:29,491 --> 00:15:30,860
I don't recognize this part of town.

461
00:15:30,861 --> 00:15:34,530
- So we're in Sanggye-dong?
- We're in Sanggye.

462
00:15:34,531 --> 00:15:35,800
We're in Sanggye-dong.

463
00:15:35,801 --> 00:15:37,371
(They will spend their first spare time in Sanggye-dong.)

464
00:15:37,571 --> 00:15:41,070
I remember seeing Paik Hospital somewhere around here.

465
00:15:41,071 --> 00:15:43,740
- A hospital?
- There was a big hospital in Nowon.

466
00:15:43,741 --> 00:15:45,911
- Sanggye Paik Hospital? It's here.
- Yes.

467
00:15:46,881 --> 00:15:48,540
(Remembering something)

468
00:15:48,541 --> 00:15:50,881
When Chun Hee was on "Family Outing,"

469
00:15:51,311 --> 00:15:54,880
he washed the radish with laundry soap.

470
00:15:54,881 --> 00:15:56,321
People talked about that a lot.

471
00:15:56,451 --> 00:15:57,490
The radish?

472
00:15:57,491 --> 00:15:59,491
- Yes.
- Everyone forgot about it. Why...

473
00:16:01,021 --> 00:16:03,960
(It was the days of his Chunderella era.)

474
00:16:03,961 --> 00:16:06,460
You know what? I'm going to show you...

475
00:16:06,461 --> 00:16:07,800
(What?)

476
00:16:07,801 --> 00:16:09,771
(No way. Is he washing it with soap?)

477
00:16:11,131 --> 00:16:12,301
Chun Hee!

478
00:16:13,641 --> 00:16:15,341
(Puzzled)

479
00:16:18,041 --> 00:16:19,240
(Hold on. Stop!)

480
00:16:19,241 --> 00:16:21,110
(The birth of a legendary scene...)

481
00:16:21,111 --> 00:16:22,980
(that people still talk about after 18 years)

482
00:16:22,981 --> 00:16:25,080
- We harvested it from the field.
- Listen.

483
00:16:25,081 --> 00:16:26,350
- He washed it with the soap.
- They...

484
00:16:26,351 --> 00:16:28,220
- They told me to wash it well.
- Wait.

485
00:16:28,221 --> 00:16:31,550
A friend of mine recently told me about this story.

486
00:16:31,551 --> 00:16:33,120
I told her I was going to be on this show with Chun Hee.

487
00:16:33,121 --> 00:16:35,320
She said, "Wait. Didn't he become famous..."

488
00:16:35,321 --> 00:16:38,030
"for washing an ingredient with dish soap?"

489
00:16:38,031 --> 00:16:39,330
- He's famous for that.
- I said, "No way."

490
00:16:39,331 --> 00:16:40,630
"He didn't do that."

491
00:16:40,631 --> 00:16:43,170
If he did that now, it would've gone viral on social media.

492
00:16:43,171 --> 00:16:44,600
(The legendary Chunderella actually did that.)

493
00:16:44,601 --> 00:16:46,271
- The soap looked...
- How old were you back then?

494
00:16:46,401 --> 00:16:49,940
I was 29 or 30 years old back then.

495
00:16:49,941 --> 00:16:51,971
- He was old enough.
- Soo Ro... Come on.

496
00:16:52,541 --> 00:16:55,681
After that, people talked about Chun Hee a lot.

497
00:16:56,081 --> 00:16:57,780
It's on the fourth floor of this building.

498
00:16:57,781 --> 00:16:58,951
Which one?

499
00:16:59,011 --> 00:17:00,880
This one? Building 744?

500
00:17:00,881 --> 00:17:01,881
(Our company offers great benefits.)

501
00:17:01,882 --> 00:17:03,190
- Building 744.
- Here...

502
00:17:03,191 --> 00:17:04,950
You're great at finding places.

503
00:17:04,951 --> 00:17:06,921
- Fourth floor. It's a pharmacy.
- It's a pharmacy.

504
00:17:08,061 --> 00:17:10,130
- A pharmacy.
- It's a pharmacy.

505
00:17:10,131 --> 00:17:11,830
(Getting to see the owner is a benefit of working here?)

506
00:17:11,831 --> 00:17:14,101
My goodness. Hello. This is the place.

507
00:17:14,401 --> 00:17:15,900
- It must be her son.
- I see.

508
00:17:15,901 --> 00:17:17,700
- Hello.
- Oh, my!

509
00:17:17,701 --> 00:17:18,701
- Hello.
- Hello.

510
00:17:18,702 --> 00:17:20,801
- Your son is the pharmacist?
- Yes.

511
00:17:20,901 --> 00:17:22,340
- Right?
- Hello.

512
00:17:22,341 --> 00:17:23,670
- Are you Myung Seon?
- Yes!

513
00:17:23,671 --> 00:17:26,080
- I see.
- Nice to meet you.

514
00:17:26,081 --> 00:17:27,640
- Hello.
- Hello.

515
00:17:27,641 --> 00:17:29,511
(First Whenever Guests: Kim Myung Seon and Yoon Je Hoon)

516
00:17:29,811 --> 00:17:31,850
So the owner is...

517
00:17:31,851 --> 00:17:33,720
- Our guess was right.
- We were right.

518
00:17:33,721 --> 00:17:34,980
(A warm and neat pharmacy run by her pharmacist son)

519
00:17:34,981 --> 00:17:38,890
Actually, we guessed who you might be on our way here.

520
00:17:38,891 --> 00:17:40,320
- You're his mom, right?
- Yes.

521
00:17:40,321 --> 00:17:42,730
- You're her son and the pharmacist.
- Yes.

522
00:17:42,731 --> 00:17:45,090
How long have you been running this pharmacy?

523
00:17:45,091 --> 00:17:48,701
- It's been 3 years.
- I see. It's been 3 years.

524
00:17:48,761 --> 00:17:52,230
How did you end up working together in the pharmacy?

525
00:17:52,231 --> 00:17:54,640
He used to work at someone else's pharmacy.

526
00:17:54,641 --> 00:17:56,210
He said he was going to open his own pharmacy.

527
00:17:56,211 --> 00:17:57,771
And I was taking a break for 3 or 4 years then.

528
00:17:58,041 --> 00:17:59,910
I wanted to help him.

529
00:17:59,911 --> 00:18:02,750
So I asked him if I could work at his pharmacy.

530
00:18:02,751 --> 00:18:04,380
He had to hire someone anyway.

531
00:18:04,381 --> 00:18:05,611
So that's how it started.

532
00:18:06,051 --> 00:18:08,880
Je Hoon, how is it for you? You're working...

533
00:18:08,881 --> 00:18:10,690
- with your mother.
- Yes.

534
00:18:10,691 --> 00:18:13,721
Well, there are pros and cons of working together.

535
00:18:13,891 --> 00:18:17,090
My mother takes good care of...

536
00:18:17,091 --> 00:18:19,130
- our older customers.
- Right.

537
00:18:19,131 --> 00:18:21,460
- That's good.
- She's good with customers.

538
00:18:21,461 --> 00:18:22,501
But...

539
00:18:22,731 --> 00:18:24,131
she doesn't listen to me.

540
00:18:24,271 --> 00:18:26,170
- So I'm her boss.
- You're her boss.

541
00:18:26,171 --> 00:18:27,371
But she doesn't listen to me.

542
00:18:27,671 --> 00:18:29,241
- Gosh.
- My gosh.

543
00:18:29,571 --> 00:18:30,770
She doesn't listen to me.

544
00:18:30,771 --> 00:18:32,780
Looking at just the facts,

545
00:18:32,781 --> 00:18:34,410
- she's your employee.
- Right.

546
00:18:34,411 --> 00:18:36,080
- It's hard to control her.
- Right.

547
00:18:36,081 --> 00:18:38,050
So we fight a lot.

548
00:18:38,051 --> 00:18:39,551
What do you usually fight about?

549
00:18:39,981 --> 00:18:43,291
Well, we have to display health supplements.

550
00:18:43,821 --> 00:18:45,090
He and I have a different way...

551
00:18:45,091 --> 00:18:46,361
- of displaying them.
- How?

552
00:18:46,891 --> 00:18:48,460
He would tell me to do it in a certain way.

553
00:18:48,461 --> 00:18:50,061
But I'd do it my way.

554
00:18:50,461 --> 00:18:53,001
Then he'd get angry at that.

555
00:18:53,331 --> 00:18:55,460
We fight over such small things every day.

556
00:18:55,461 --> 00:18:58,631
- You're her boss and her son.
- Yes.

557
00:18:58,831 --> 00:19:01,041
How do you want to display the health supplements?

558
00:19:01,271 --> 00:19:04,240
When I tell other employees to do it this way,

559
00:19:04,241 --> 00:19:06,181
they say "Okay" and do it my way.

560
00:19:06,411 --> 00:19:09,241
But I have to explain to her why I want it that way.

561
00:19:10,081 --> 00:19:12,651
Did you have a job before working here?

562
00:19:12,851 --> 00:19:15,350
Back in the day, I used to run...

563
00:19:15,351 --> 00:19:16,621
- the famous bakery franchise.
- The bakery.

564
00:19:16,851 --> 00:19:18,721
- She ran a Paris Baguette branch.
- Paris Baguette.

565
00:19:19,091 --> 00:19:20,220
- Back then, she was the boss, - That...

566
00:19:20,221 --> 00:19:21,321
and I was an employee.

567
00:19:21,921 --> 00:19:24,690
I can't stop thinking like the owner.

568
00:19:24,691 --> 00:19:27,130
It's hard for me to keep my work and personal life separate.

569
00:19:27,131 --> 00:19:28,800
That explains why you want to display things...

570
00:19:28,801 --> 00:19:30,031
- Right.
- in a certain way.

571
00:19:30,371 --> 00:19:32,571
I'm very confident when it comes to displaying things.

572
00:19:32,971 --> 00:19:34,370
There are specific rules...

573
00:19:34,371 --> 00:19:35,741
- in displaying bread.
- Of course.

574
00:19:35,941 --> 00:19:37,241
- But we're not displaying bread.
- Locations...

575
00:19:37,441 --> 00:19:39,941
- I did that for 11 years.
- To encourage people to buy...

576
00:19:40,311 --> 00:19:41,580
That's understandable...

577
00:19:41,581 --> 00:19:42,850
- since she ran a business before.
- Yes.

578
00:19:42,851 --> 00:19:44,680
She's an employee, but she works...

579
00:19:44,681 --> 00:19:47,220
- as if she owns the place.
- Right. That's it.

580
00:19:47,221 --> 00:19:49,851
- So two owners are fighting.
- So there are two owners.

581
00:19:49,951 --> 00:19:52,520
But if you hire someone else for her job,

582
00:19:52,521 --> 00:19:53,720
you won't be happy with them.

583
00:19:53,721 --> 00:19:56,461
- No one can replace her.
- No. I think...

584
00:19:56,761 --> 00:20:00,131
- No?
- Wait. Why...

585
00:20:00,561 --> 00:20:02,561
- He disagreed.
- He disagreed.

586
00:20:03,131 --> 00:20:04,371
- Well, - Wait.

587
00:20:04,931 --> 00:20:06,100
Je Hoon.

588
00:20:06,101 --> 00:20:07,570
(Shocked by his unique answer)

589
00:20:07,571 --> 00:20:10,010
There are upsides of working together,

590
00:20:10,011 --> 00:20:11,940
but there also must be some downsides.

591
00:20:11,941 --> 00:20:14,981
The upside is that I get to see my son all day.

592
00:20:15,581 --> 00:20:16,680
But the downside is...

593
00:20:16,681 --> 00:20:19,011
- What is it?
- that we're together the whole day.

594
00:20:19,181 --> 00:20:20,351
Oh, no.

595
00:20:21,681 --> 00:20:24,921
I'm sorry. Did I say that out loud? I was only thinking that.

596
00:20:25,221 --> 00:20:27,221
I was thinking the same thing too.

597
00:20:27,691 --> 00:20:29,020
I was thinking the same thing just now.

598
00:20:29,021 --> 00:20:30,061
(Working together is hard for both the mother and son.)

599
00:20:30,391 --> 00:20:31,891
- It's great.
- Yes.

600
00:20:32,331 --> 00:20:34,331
He's my boss and my son at the same time.

601
00:20:34,831 --> 00:20:36,370
It's so hard.

602
00:20:36,371 --> 00:20:37,371
- I bet.
- Yes.

603
00:20:37,372 --> 00:20:40,440
But you pack a boxed meal for him every day?

604
00:20:40,441 --> 00:20:42,140
Yes. Young people these days...

605
00:20:42,141 --> 00:20:43,841
- eat out a lot. You know?
- Yes.

606
00:20:44,011 --> 00:20:46,411
So I bring a boxed meal every day.

607
00:20:48,111 --> 00:20:53,080
(The biggest benefit is the meal prepared with love from his mom.)

608
00:20:53,081 --> 00:20:55,080
Well, your mother...

609
00:20:55,081 --> 00:20:57,350
- brings lunch every day.
- Yes.

610
00:20:57,351 --> 00:20:59,490
She wrote that she gets upset when you don't finish your food.

611
00:20:59,491 --> 00:21:01,921
Yes. Ever since I was little,

612
00:21:02,021 --> 00:21:04,561
she always gave me more than I could eat.

613
00:21:04,691 --> 00:21:05,960
No.

614
00:21:05,961 --> 00:21:08,531
I always give him one scoop of rice.

615
00:21:09,261 --> 00:21:11,970
- I always pack one scoop of rice.
- They're saying different things.

616
00:21:11,971 --> 00:21:14,240
- Could it be a big scoop?
- No.

617
00:21:14,241 --> 00:21:15,500
I'm serious.

618
00:21:15,501 --> 00:21:16,740
- It's half of what I eat.
- Did you...

619
00:21:16,741 --> 00:21:18,010
scoop again for more rice...

620
00:21:18,011 --> 00:21:19,270
- after scooping it?
- No.

621
00:21:19,271 --> 00:21:21,180
She fills up a side dish container with rice.

622
00:21:21,181 --> 00:21:23,011
No. That's not true.

623
00:21:23,251 --> 00:21:24,251
No?

624
00:21:24,252 --> 00:21:26,451
- She said no.
- She...

625
00:21:26,821 --> 00:21:28,921
When I eat at my parents' house,

626
00:21:29,151 --> 00:21:31,320
I can't seem to finish everything.

627
00:21:31,321 --> 00:21:34,091
- Right.
- I always leave 1 or 2 spoonfuls.

628
00:21:34,421 --> 00:21:36,891
There are many days I just can't finish everything.

629
00:21:37,191 --> 00:21:39,231
- He never finishes everything.
- Really?

630
00:21:39,361 --> 00:21:40,600
He always leaves food.

631
00:21:40,601 --> 00:21:42,130
(That's how every mother feels.)

632
00:21:42,131 --> 00:21:45,031
So your son is paying now. Right?

633
00:21:45,271 --> 00:21:47,241
- It's just some pocket money.
- I pay her a lot.

634
00:21:49,341 --> 00:21:50,440
- This too?
- Gosh.

635
00:21:50,441 --> 00:21:51,670
- Their stories don't match again.
- They're saying different things...

636
00:21:51,671 --> 00:21:52,940
about the salary too.

637
00:21:52,941 --> 00:21:54,240
- Who's telling the truth?
- Right.

638
00:21:54,241 --> 00:21:56,140
- She says it's not much.
- Well...

639
00:21:56,141 --> 00:21:57,780
I contribute a lot to this pharmacy.

640
00:21:57,781 --> 00:21:59,580
- I work really hard.
- I see. Compared to that?

641
00:21:59,581 --> 00:22:02,380
- Compared to that...
- She does contribute a lot here.

642
00:22:02,381 --> 00:22:04,720
- Of course.
- She has to pack food.

643
00:22:04,721 --> 00:22:07,890
- It costs money to buy ingredients.
- Of course.

644
00:22:07,891 --> 00:22:11,091
- A meal costs over 10 dollars now.
- You can't find meals under 10.

645
00:22:11,161 --> 00:22:12,230
You totally get me.

646
00:22:12,231 --> 00:22:14,600
So you don't eat out on the days she's working here.

647
00:22:14,601 --> 00:22:17,070
- You don't order food.
- No. But after lunch,

648
00:22:17,071 --> 00:22:18,600
- he orders coffee for me.
- Yes.

649
00:22:18,601 --> 00:22:20,371
- I see. Coffee.
- Yes.

650
00:22:20,741 --> 00:22:23,271
But I can't get the drink I want.

651
00:22:23,671 --> 00:22:25,570
He tells me I shouldn't have sweet coffee.

652
00:22:25,571 --> 00:22:27,810
- That's for your health.
- It's for your health.

653
00:22:27,811 --> 00:22:29,680
When I eat something sweet,

654
00:22:29,681 --> 00:22:31,380
he somehow finds out and takes it away.

655
00:22:31,381 --> 00:22:32,810
He does that even if it's in my mouth.

656
00:22:32,811 --> 00:22:34,050
- Really?
- Yes.

657
00:22:34,051 --> 00:22:35,520
He forbids me from eating it.

658
00:22:35,521 --> 00:22:37,151
- She...
- Does she have high blood sugar?

659
00:22:37,621 --> 00:22:39,191
She's prediabetic.

660
00:22:39,321 --> 00:22:41,690
- Then you need to be careful.
- It's understandable.

661
00:22:41,691 --> 00:22:43,830
I get that he's looking out for me.

662
00:22:43,831 --> 00:22:45,991
But it doesn't feel good.

663
00:22:46,131 --> 00:22:48,801
- Parents don't like to be nagged.
- It doesn't feel good.

664
00:22:50,331 --> 00:22:51,701
- We even had a scuffle.
- It's very annoying.

665
00:22:52,071 --> 00:22:53,741
- What?
- She kicked me.

666
00:22:54,871 --> 00:22:57,210
- In the pharmacy?
- You guys fought over sweets?

667
00:22:57,211 --> 00:22:58,240
- In the back?
- Yes.

668
00:22:58,241 --> 00:22:59,740
- They're funny.
- Yes.

669
00:22:59,741 --> 00:23:02,041
When she told me that the staff picked us for the show,

670
00:23:02,741 --> 00:23:04,281
she kept telling me...

671
00:23:04,551 --> 00:23:06,210
to be funny during the interview.

672
00:23:06,211 --> 00:23:08,821
She was watching me and said I wasn't funny.

673
00:23:09,021 --> 00:23:11,821
I said I shouldn't try to be funny. I should tell them as they are.

674
00:23:12,051 --> 00:23:13,861
She even told me to do an impression.

675
00:23:14,491 --> 00:23:16,120
But he's really good at it.

676
00:23:16,121 --> 00:23:18,360
- Is there one you can do?
- Yes, there is.

677
00:23:18,361 --> 00:23:20,960
- No. I just do that at home.
- We never ask people...

678
00:23:20,961 --> 00:23:23,071
We're not here to audition to become comedians.

679
00:23:24,531 --> 00:23:27,300
He can do Lee Jung Jae's impression from "Assassination."

680
00:23:27,301 --> 00:23:28,570
No. Listen.

681
00:23:28,571 --> 00:23:29,810
- It's very good.
- Listen.

682
00:23:29,811 --> 00:23:31,671
She's saying your impression is great.

683
00:23:31,871 --> 00:23:33,111
I'm so curious.

684
00:23:33,341 --> 00:23:35,510
- No.
- You're already laughing.

685
00:23:35,511 --> 00:23:38,010
My goodness. They're so funny.

686
00:23:38,011 --> 00:23:39,610
You know what I observed?

687
00:23:39,611 --> 00:23:41,421
- She's full of energy.
- That's right.

688
00:23:41,551 --> 00:23:43,221
- She's so cheerful.
- Yes.

689
00:23:43,421 --> 00:23:44,721
She's energetic.

690
00:23:44,921 --> 00:23:46,221
My goodness.

691
00:23:46,721 --> 00:23:49,021
So what made you write to our show?

692
00:23:49,161 --> 00:23:51,591
- She's a huge fan of the show.
- Yes.

693
00:23:51,791 --> 00:23:54,101
- She even bought the egg flat.
- Really?

694
00:23:54,301 --> 00:23:55,930
When she sees your games, she tries them too.

695
00:23:55,931 --> 00:23:57,901
My family loves playing games.

696
00:23:58,101 --> 00:23:59,670
We play yutnori...

697
00:23:59,671 --> 00:24:00,870
- whenever we meet up.
- Really?

698
00:24:00,871 --> 00:24:02,540
A long time ago, you guys played this game...

699
00:24:02,541 --> 00:24:04,470
- with the egg flat.
- Yes, we did that.

700
00:24:04,471 --> 00:24:05,710
I also bought that...

701
00:24:05,711 --> 00:24:06,810
- to play with my family.
- To play the game?

702
00:24:06,811 --> 00:24:08,081
- Yes.
- So you tried it?

703
00:24:08,181 --> 00:24:09,341
It was fun.

704
00:24:09,851 --> 00:24:12,751
- It's not easy once you try it.
- It's not easy.

705
00:24:12,981 --> 00:24:14,020
- Right.
- You get nervous.

706
00:24:14,021 --> 00:24:16,281
I'm a huge fan of you guys.

707
00:24:16,621 --> 00:24:18,650
It feels like I'm dreaming right now.

708
00:24:18,651 --> 00:24:21,491
Do you know what I always say about you, Jae Seok?

709
00:24:21,661 --> 00:24:24,290
I say Dr. Oh Eun Yeong and you...

710
00:24:24,291 --> 00:24:26,191
should be designated as a national treasure.

711
00:24:26,561 --> 00:24:29,061
I always say that to my family.

712
00:24:29,431 --> 00:24:31,700
- You're such a hard worker.
- Gosh, no.

713
00:24:31,701 --> 00:24:34,470
- You set a good example.
- No.

714
00:24:34,471 --> 00:24:36,470
- You have no flaws.
- That's not true.

715
00:24:36,471 --> 00:24:38,270
I really hope we succeed today.

716
00:24:38,271 --> 00:24:40,480
- We need to give them presents.
- They need to get presents today.

717
00:24:40,481 --> 00:24:42,181
If my family hears this...

718
00:24:42,341 --> 00:24:44,710
- Okay.
- No. You're a great man. I mean it.

719
00:24:44,711 --> 00:24:46,851
No. My gosh.

720
00:24:47,321 --> 00:24:50,620
- All right. Since we're here today, - Yes.

721
00:24:50,621 --> 00:24:53,291
I hope we can give you many presents.

722
00:24:53,461 --> 00:24:56,931
I'm feeling some pressure today.

723
00:24:57,061 --> 00:24:59,091
- Let's start with Round One.
- Okay.

724
00:25:00,261 --> 00:25:01,331
All right.

725
00:25:01,731 --> 00:25:02,588
Read it, please.

726
00:25:03,871 --> 00:25:06,370
"This will complete the benefit for the mom."

727
00:25:06,371 --> 00:25:07,740
"Free coffee from the son."

728
00:25:07,741 --> 00:25:09,171
"Illy coffee machine."

729
00:25:09,741 --> 00:25:11,241
- We need it.
- That's...

730
00:25:11,671 --> 00:25:13,611
- Coffee machine.
- Coffee machine.

731
00:25:13,741 --> 00:25:15,010
We really need it.

732
00:25:15,011 --> 00:25:16,341
- Coffee machine.
- Oh, my.

733
00:25:16,711 --> 00:25:18,011
- A coffee machine.
- That's great.

734
00:25:18,111 --> 00:25:20,251
- That looks great.
- That's great.

